--TEST--
`$a[]` lands on the append counter's last usable key and refuses the one past it, catchably — ADR 0007 § 5
--FILE--
<?mwl
// The counter `mwl_runtime::array`'s `Table::note_index` keeps saturates at
// `i64::MAX`, so the bound named here is the last key an append can land on
// and the first append that has nowhere to go. Both sides, per conventions.md:
// a case asserting only the refusal still passes against an implementation
// that stops one entry early.

// The last accepted side. A key one below the maximum leaves the next element
// free, so `$a[]` lands exactly on the maximum.
array<int> $accepted = [];
$accepted[9223372036854775806] = 1;
$accepted[] = 2;
echo "landed at: ", Core\Json::encode(Core\Arr::keys($accepted)), "\n";

// The first refused side. The counter now names a live key, and PHP 8.5 throws
// there rather than overwriting it — `mwl_runtime::mwl_array_append` carries
// that message verbatim, and it is the one array write with a fault channel to
// carry it in.
try {
    $accepted[] = 3;
    echo "appended past the bound\n";
} catch (Throwable $refused) {
    echo "refused: ", $refused->message, "\n";
}

// The refusal changed nothing: nothing overwritten, nothing added.
echo "count: ", Core\Arr::count($accepted), "\n";
echo "still holds: ", $accepted[9223372036854775807], "\n";

// It is a `LogicError` — spec § 10's class for a program asking for something
// its own state forbids — not the bare `RuntimeError` a helper with nothing
// more specific to say raises.
try {
    $accepted[] = 4;
    echo "appended past the bound\n";
} catch (LogicError $classified) {
    echo "class: LogicError\n";
}

// Writing the bound itself refuses at the very first append, because that key
// is what moved the counter onto itself.
array<int> $atBound = [];
$atBound[9223372036854775807] = 1;
try {
    $atBound[] = 2;
    echo "appended onto the bound\n";
} catch (Throwable $atMax) {
    echo "refused: ", $atMax->message, "\n";
}

// `unset` frees the key without moving the counter — the sibling case
// `unset-does-not-move-the-append-counter.mwlt` pins that half — so the key it
// freed is exactly where the next append lands, a second time. PHP 8.5 agrees.
unset($atBound[9223372036854775807]);
$atBound[] = 2;
echo "after unset: ", Core\Json::encode(Core\Arr::keys($atBound)), "\n";
echo "count: ", Core\Arr::count($atBound), "\n";
--EXPECT--
landed at: ["9223372036854775806","9223372036854775807"]
refused: Cannot add element to the array as the next element is already occupied
count: 2
still holds: 2
class: LogicError
refused: Cannot add element to the array as the next element is already occupied
after unset: ["9223372036854775807"]
count: 1
